The hivemind's database schema.  The hivemind brings the database up to date itself when it starts,
before anything else uses it (see spns/migrations.hpp); nothing here is applied by hand.  The files
are built into the hivemind (cmake/SpnsSchema.cmake), so it reads none of them at run time, and
adding, removing or editing one re-runs CMake.

full_schema.sql is the schema as it stands after every migration here, in one piece: it is what to
read to see the current schema.  A database with none of the migrations recorded, and none of the
hivemind's tables, is created from it in one step, and every migration is recorded as applied
without being run.  It must be kept in step with the migrations: built from it alone and from the
migrations alone, a database must come out the same, which the e2e "schema" scenario checks.  Column
order counts in that comparison, so a column that a migration adds goes last in its table here too.

Every other file here beginning with a number and ending with .sql is a one-time migration, run in
order against the `migrations_applied` table: if its name is not there, it is run, and its name
inserted, in one transaction, which is rolled back if it fails (and the hivemind does not start).
A migration does not begin a transaction of its own.  Each run happens under an advisory lock, so
two hiveminds starting at once cannot both apply one.

The order is by migration *name* -- the filename without its .sql, which is what is recorded in
`migrations_applied` -- not by filename.  This matters when one name is a prefix of another: sorting
filenames would order `001_foo+002.sql` before `001_foo.sql`, because '+' sorts below '.'.  Sorting
names makes it a prefix comparison, so the shorter name always runs first.

Name migrations `NNN_description_of_migration.sql`, such as `003_device_tables.sql`.  A migration
that depends on earlier ones must use a larger `NNN` than they do.

000 to 002 are written to run against a database made before migrations were recorded, which has the
tables but no `migrations_applied`: each statement is a no-op where its change is there already.
Such a database has them run and recorded (not full_schema.sql, as it has tables already).  Later
migrations need not be idempotent, as they only ever run once, against a recorded database.


Iterating on a branch
---------------------

While developing a schema change you generally want to keep updating a database you have already
been using, rather than recreating it after every edit.  To do that, name the first migration what
the finished one will be called, and add addenda alongside it as you go:

    003_new_feature.sql
    003_new_feature+001.sql
    003_new_feature+002.sql

Before merging, fold the addenda back into `003_new_feature.sql` and delete them.  Because your
database recorded `003_new_feature` when you first created that file, the squashed migration is
already applied and is not re-run: your development database survives the squash.  The orphaned
`003_new_feature+NNN` rows left in `migrations_applied` are ignored, since only names present in the
registry are ever consulted.

If instead you name the first file something you later rename, the squash leaves your development
database out of step and you have to recreate it -- a nuisance rather than a problem, but easily
avoided.
